<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Germanicus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15 BCE–19 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Germanicus Julius Caesar · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julio-claudian-dynasty">Julio-Claudian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Germanicus Julius Caesar (15 BCE–19 CE) was a Roman general, nephew and adopted son of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. From 14 to 16 CE he led campaigns across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, visiting the site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg</a> disaster and recovering two of Varus' lost eagles, but made no lasting conquest. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> after a quarrel with its governor Piso, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> later became emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/germanicus.jpg" alt="Germanicus" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. Paul Getty Museum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Germanicus,_front_-_Getty_Museum_(2021.66).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Germanicus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 24 May 15 BCE to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero-claudius-drusus">Nero Claudius Drusus</a> and Antonia the Younger, and his younger siblings were Livilla and the future emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>. His grandparents included the triumvir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Octavia Minor on his mother's side. His father's victories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> earned the name Germanicus, which was awarded to the father after his death in 9 BCE. In 4 CE his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> adopted him, and he entered the gens Julia as Germanicus Julius Caesar. He married Agrippina the Elder, a granddaughter of Augustus, and was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> and the maternal grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He became quaestor in 7 CE, five years before the legal age, and was sent to Illyricum to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> suppress the Pannonian and Dalmatian rebellion, where he defeated the Mazaei. In 12 CE he was consul, and in 13 CE Augustus appointed him commander on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, with eight legions, about one third of the Roman army. When Augustus died in 14 CE the legions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhine">Lower Rhine</a> mutinied over unpaid bonuses, and Germanicus dealt with them. Their attempt to proclaim him emperor failed, since he stayed loyal and led them against the German tribes instead.</p><p>He campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> from 14 to 16 CE, avenged the defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a> and recovered two of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> eagles lost there. On 26 May 17 he held a triumph in which Thusnelda, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>, and her three-year-old son were led among the captives, although Arminius himself was never taken. In 18 CE he was given command in the east with imperium maius, reorganised the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and incorporated Cappadocia and Commagene.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the east he quarrelled with Gnaeus Calpurnius Piso, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> had appointed. Germanicus fell ill at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, became convinced that Piso was poisoning him, and renounced their friendship. He died on 10 October 19. Several sources blamed Piso, acting on the orders of Tiberius, but this was never proven. Piso died before his trial in the Senate ended, ostensibly by suicide, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> supposes that Tiberius may have had him killed.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> described Germanicus as a fine general, kind and temperate. The historian Anthony A. Barrett accepts that he was an able general. Ancient writers often contrasted him as a tragic hero with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> as a tyrant. In 4 CE Germanicus also wrote a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> version of Aratus's Phainomena, which survives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Germanicus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Senators were forbidden to visit Egypt without the emperor's permission, and Tiberius rebuked his nephew <b>Germanicus</b> for touring the country in 19 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Tacitus mentions a broad earthwork they had built to mark their boundary with the Cherusci; in 16 CE <b>Germanicus</b> fought the Battle of the Angrivarian Wall there after his victory at Idistaviso.</blockquote><a href="/state/angrivarii">Angrivarii</a></li><li><blockquote>In 9 CE they joined Arminius against Varus and captured the eagle of the Nineteenth Legion, which <b>Germanicus</b>' general Stertinius recovered in 15.</blockquote><a href="/state/bructeri">Bructeri</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Germanicus</b> burned their chief centre, Mattium, in 15 CE, but Rome never brought them under lasting control.</blockquote><a href="/state/chatti">Chatti</a></li><li><blockquote>He then fought <b>Germanicus</b> to a standstill in 15 to 16 and defeated Maroboduus in 17, before being murdered by kinsmen around 21.</blockquote><a href="/state/cherusci">Cherusci</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=7" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Germanicus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=7">What was here in 7 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/nero-claudius-drusus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nero-claudius-drusus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Nero Claudius Drusus</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; 38–9 BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/caligula"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/caligula.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Caligula</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 12–41 CE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Were I succumbing to nature, I should have just ground of complaint even against the gods for thus tearing me away in my youth by an untimely death from parents, children, country.&#8221;</p><footer>Tacitus, Annals, book 2 &#183; tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb, 1876 &#183; his words to his friends as he lay dying at Antioch, 19 CE, as Tacitus gives them &#183; <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Tacitus/annals.mb.txt" rel="noopener">The Internet Classics Archive</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is not the chief duty of friends to follow the dead with unprofitable laments, but to remember his wishes, to fulfil his commands.&#8221;</p><footer>Tacitus, Annals, book 2 &#183; tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb, 1876 &#183; his words to his friends as he lay dying at Antioch, 19 CE, as Tacitus gives them &#183; <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Tacitus/annals.mb.txt" rel="noopener">The Internet Classics Archive</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
